     You need to put the '-u' option before '-D' since the name supplied
there is what nethack on a single user system uses to decide whether
or not to honor '-D'.  If it decides not to honor that then you'll start
out in explore mode instead.  I have no idea whether explore mode adds
any menu entries on the Windows version; unlike debug mode, explore mode
does not add any extra commands.

     As someone else mentioned, the list shown by the '?' command has an
extra entry when in wizard mode and the information shown by selecting
that help option lists many of the debug commands, including <ctrl/W>
for wishing.  Others show up when you use '#' to start an extended
command and then follow with ?' instead of the starting letter(s) of a
command name.  But those are generally more obscure than the various
control-whatever keystrokes which lend themselves to experimentation.


